#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# Refuse to push a tree that fails the mechanical checks.
#
# WHY A HOOK AND NOT A HABIT. On 2026-08-10 a commit went to the gaming PC without review and
# carried three defects, one of which permanently deleted containers an NPC had walked to. The
# fix for that is not "remember to run the checks" -- it is that pushing without them is not
# possible. Every mistake here costs a game restart on ANOTHER machine to detect.
#
# WHAT THIS GATES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.
#   lint.sh   -- syntax, and every item id resolving against the 6386 the engine defines.
#   audit.py  -- the mechanical half of a review: unpaired latches, vendor citations with no
#                version folder, undefined globals (the nil-global trap that killed a whole
#                behaviour on 08-08).
#
# It does NOT gate pz-review. That is a call to a model, it needs judgement -- "does this loop
# terminate", "does the caller have a re-entry motor" -- and a hook cannot ask those. Automate
# the gate, not the review.
#
# Install:  ./tools/hooks/install.sh
# Bypass:   only with a reason you would write in the commit message.

set -u

ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$ROOT" || exit 1

fail=0

echo "pre-push: lint"
if ! ./tools/lint.sh; then
    echo "pre-push: lint FAILED" >&2
    fail=1
fi

echo "pre-push: audit"
if ! ./tools/audit.py; then
    echo "pre-push: audit FAILED" >&2
    fail=1
fi

if [ "$fail" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'MSG'

pre-push BLOCKED.

Fix what is reported above. If you genuinely need to push anyway -- a broken tree you
want backed up, a checkpoint before a rewrite -- say so explicitly:

    git push --no-verify

and put the reason in the commit message, because the next person to read the log is
going to be you, on the other machine, wondering why this shipped.
MSG
    exit 1
fi

echo "pre-push: clean"
exit 0
